+Since $(2e-s)^2=N+4$ and $\lambda\ne0$, it follows that
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:constant-sector}
+ e_A=e,\qquad \lambda_A=\lambda,\qquad C_A=-\lambda e=C.
+\end{equation}
+Thus $d=-s/e$ and a chosen square root $\nu$ of $d$ are constant nonzero
+scalars, and
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:sector-scalars}
+ d^2=N(d+1),\qquad se=-N/d,\qquad \nu^2=d.
+\end{equation}
+The remaining tangent equations include
 \begin{align}
- E(-u)h(u)+E(u)h(-u)&=0\quad(u\ne0),\label{eq:tangref}\\
- \widehat h(u)&=\lambda q(u)h(u),\label{eq:tangfour}
