-\subsection{Extending the construction over the dual numbers}
-Put $d=-s/e$ and fix a square root of $d$. These parameters are constant in
-$A$, since $e$ is constant. Form the Leavitt algebra
-$L_A=A\otimes_\C L_{2N}$.
-By Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:realization}, the formulas of
-\cite[Section~5.4]{RW2} define $A$-linear endomorphisms $\alpha_g$ and
-$\rho_A$ of $L_A$, together with the stated split fusion and duality maps.
-Their coefficients are algebraic expressions in $E_A,q,s,\lambda,d^{1/2}$;
-the denominators are units. The identity verification in the hypothesis is
-used precisely here, before any appeal to deformation rigidity. In particular,
-\[
- \rho_A^2\simeq\bigoplus_{g\in G}\alpha_g\ \oplus\ \rho_A^{\oplus N},
- \qquad \alpha_g\rho_A=\rho_A,
- \qquad \rho_A\alpha_g\simeq\rho_A.
-\]
-
-\paragraph{Flatness of morphism spaces.}
-This is the additional check that cannot be replaced by saying simply that
-``the formulas work over $A$.'' For the basic objects $\alpha_g,\rho_A$, the
-reduced category has scalar endomorphisms and zero morphisms between distinct
-simples, as computed in \cite[Section~5.4]{RW2}. Let $z=z_0+\eps z_1$ be an
-intertwiner in $L_A$. Between distinct basic simples reduction gives $z_0=0$,
-and the remaining intertwining equation says that $z_1$ is an intertwiner
-between the corresponding distinct reduced simples. Thus $z_1=0$.
-For an endomorphism of one basic simple, $z_0$ is a scalar. Subtract that
-scalar identity, which is an exact intertwiner over $A$; the same reasoning
-shows that $z_1$ is a scalar. Therefore
-\[
- \End_A(\alpha_g)=A,
- \quad\End_A(\rho_A)=A,
- \quad\Hom_A(S,T)=0\quad(S\not\simeq T)
-\]
-for these chosen basic simples. The explicit fusion decompositions extend
-this to all tensor words as free finite-rank $A$-modules. Choosing the split
-skeleton and bases of the fusion spaces therefore gives a flat deformation
-of the reduced fusion category, with the same fusion rules.
-
-\paragraph{First-order rigidity.}
-The theorem of Etingof--Nikshych--Ostrik gives
-$H^3(\mathcal C)=0$ for a fusion category in characteristic zero.
-This cohomology classifies first-order deformations of the associativity
-structure \cite[Theorem 2.27, \S7.1]{ENO}. It follows that the deformation just
-constructed is monoidally equivalent, with its reduction fixed, to the
-constant deformation $A\otimes_\C\mathcal C$.
-
-\subsection{Why gauge-triviality forces the actual coordinates to be fixed}
-The preceding paragraph alone does not show $h=0$: gauge-trivial structure
-constants can still move. We use the scalar reconstruction of
-\cite[Section~5.5]{RW2} over $A$, as required in
-Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:realization}.
-There the invariant line determining $T_0\in\Hom(\rho,\rho^2)$ is the
-trivial-character line for the $\alpha_g$ action. Over $A$ it is still free of
-rank one, since the distinct character values have invertible differences.
-The normalization of $U_g$ by its action on the rank-one space
-$\Hom(1,\rho^2)$ works over $A$ as well. Setting $T_g=U_{-g}T_0$ gives a
-basis up to multiplication by a common unit of $A$. Let $T'_g$ be the dual
-basis. The reconstruction identity is
+We will show that these infinitesimal motions vanish by constructing a
+flat deformation whose normalized categorical coordinates are $E_A(g)$.
+
+\subsection{Identities over the universal normalized coefficient ring}
+Fix $e,\lambda,d,\nu$ as above. Let
+\[
+ R=\C[Z_g:g\in G]/J,
+\]
+where $J$ is generated by $Z_0-e$, the Fourier relations
+$\widehat Z(g)-\lambda q(g)Z_g$, the reflection relations
+$q(g)Z_gZ_{-g}-1$ for $g\ne0$, and the raw equations with
+$C=-\lambda e$. Write $E_R(g)$ for the class of $Z_g$.
+We do not take the radical of $J$. Every normalized dual-number solution
+in this sector factors through $R$. All denominators used below belong
+to $\C^\times$; also every $E_R(g)$ is a unit by reflection and $E_R(0)=e$.
+
+The following finite Fourier identities hold in $R$:
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:ring-fourier-reflection}
+ \widehat E_R(g)=\lambda q(g)E_R(g),\qquad
+ q(g)E_R(g)E_R(-g)=1+se\delta_{g,0}.
+\end{equation}
+The Fourier transform on $R^G$ is invertible: character orthogonality is
+an identity in the constant coefficient field. In particular, every use
+of Fourier inversion below is valid before reducedness is known.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Correlation and dual pentagon]\label{lem:ring-correlation}
+In $R$, for every $a\in G$,
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:ring-correlation}
+ K_R(a):=\sum_t q(t)E_R(-t)E_R(t+a)
+       =N\delta_{a,0}-\frac Nd.
+\end{equation}
+The dual pentagon takes the form
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:ring-dual-pentagon}
+ q(x-y)E_R(x)E_R(y)
+ =-\lambda^{-1}e+
+ \frac1s\sum_t q(y-t)E_R(y-t)E_R(t)q(x-t)E_R(x-t).
+\end{equation}
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Taking the normalized Fourier transform in $a$ gives
+\[
+ \widehat K_R(\xi)
+ =s\lambda^{-1}E_R(-\xi)\widehat E_R(\xi)
+ =s+Ne\delta_{\xi,0}.
+\]
+Fourier inversion gives $K_R(a)=N\delta_{a,0}+se$, proving
+\eqref{eq:ring-correlation}.
+
+For the second identity, use the finite Fourier transformation in the proof
+of \cite[Theorem~5(iv)]{RW2}. More explicitly, set
+$\widetilde E_R=qE_R$ and $\widetilde C=\lambda^{-2}C=-\lambda^{-1}e$.
+Multiply the proposed dual equation by $\chi(y,w)$ and sum in $y$ with
+measure $1/s$. Using
+$\widehat{\widetilde E_R}(u)=\lambda^{-1}E_R(-u)$, it becomes
+\[
+ \lambda^{-1}q(x)E_R(x)E_R(w-x)
+ =s\widetilde C\delta_{w,0}
+ +\frac{\lambda^{-1}E_R(w)}s
+   \sum_zE_R(x-z)E_R(z)\chi(z,w)q(x-z).
+\]
+After $z\mapsto x-z$, multiplication by $\lambda$ and the elementary
+bicharacter identities, this is the Fourier transform of the raw
+pentagon equation in the other coordinate. All transformations are
+invertible over $R$. No complex conjugation or assertion about the zero
+set of $J$ is used.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Universal Leavitt realization]\label{prop:ring-realization}
+In $L_{2N}(R)=R\otimes_\C L_{2N}$, the formulas of
+\cite[Section~5.4]{RW2} define $R$-linear automorphisms $\alpha_g$, units
+$U_g$, and a unital endomorphism $\rho_R$. They satisfy
+\begin{gather}
+ \alpha_g\alpha_h=\alpha_{g+h},\quad U_gU_h=U_{g+h},\quad
+ \alpha_g\rho_R=\rho_R,\quad
+ \rho_R\alpha_g=\operatorname{Ad}(U_g)\rho_R,\label{eq:ring-covariance}\\
+ \rho_R^2(x)=\sum_gS_g\alpha_g(x)S_g'
+                   +\sum_gT_g\rho_R(x)T_g'\qquad(x\in L_{2N}(R)),
+ \label{eq:ring-fusion}\\
+ \rho_R(dS_0')S_0=1,\qquad dS_0'\rho_R(S_0)=1.
+ \label{eq:ring-snake}
+\end{gather}
+The $S_g,T_g$ and their primed partners give split fusion maps.
+All these identities commute with homomorphisms of coefficient rings.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Here the Leavitt generators obey
+\[
+ S_g'S_h=T_g'T_h=\delta_{g,h},\qquad S_g'T_h=T_g'S_h=0,\qquad
+ P_S+P_T=1,
+\]
+where $P_S=\sum_gS_gS_g'$ and $P_T=\sum_gT_gT_g'$.
+For clarity we record the formulas, in our notation:
+\begin{align*}
+ \alpha_g(S_h)&=S_{g+h},& \alpha_g(S_h')&=S_{g+h}',\\
+ \alpha_g(T_h)&=\chi(g,h)^{-1}T_h,&
+ \alpha_g(T_h')&=\chi(g,h)T_h',\\
+ U_g&=\sum_h\chi(g,h)^{-1}S_hS_h'+\sum_hT_{h-g}T_h'.
+\end{align*}
+The generator images of $\rho_R$, writing $E=E_R$ in this proof, are
+\begin{align}
+ \rho_R(S_g)&=U_g\left(d^{-1}\sum_hS_h+
+                    \nu^{-1}\sum_hq(h)T_hT_{-h}\right)U_{-g},\notag\\
+ \rho_R(S_g')&=U_g\left(d^{-1}\sum_hS_h'+
+                    \nu^{-1}\sum_hq(h)^{-1}T_{-h}'T_h'\right)U_{-g},
+ \label{eq:ring-rho-S}\\
+ \rho_R(T_g)&=\frac{\lambda}{\nu s}\sum_{h,k}\chi(h-g,k)S_hT_k'
+ +\frac{\lambda^{-1}}{q(g)s}\sum_{h,k}\chi(g+h,k)^{-1}T_kS_hS_h'
+ \notag\\
+ &\quad+\frac1s\sum_{h,k}q(h)E(h+g)\chi(g,k)^{-1}T_{h+k}T_{-h}T_k',
+ \label{eq:ring-rho-T}\\
+ \rho_R(T_g')&=\frac{\lambda^{-1}}{\nu s}\sum_{h,k}\chi(h-g,k)^{-1}T_kS_h'
+ +\frac{\lambda q(g)}s\sum_{h,k}\chi(g+h,k)S_hS_h'T_k'
+ \notag\\
+ &\quad+\frac1s\sum_{h,k}\frac{q(g+h)}{q(h)}E(-h-g)\chi(g,k)
+                    T_kT_{-h}'T_{h+k}'.\label{eq:ring-rho-Tprime}
+\end{align}
+
+\paragraph{Generator relations and completeness.}
+The four primed--unprimed products in Appendix~B.1 of \cite{RW2} reduce
+using character orthogonality, \eqref{eq:ring-fourier-reflection}, and
+\eqref{eq:ring-correlation}. For example,
+\[
+ \rho_R(S_a')\rho_R(S_b)
+ =U_a\left(d^{-2}\sum_h\chi(a-b,h)+\frac Nd\delta_{a,b}\right)U_{-b}
+ =\delta_{a,b},
+\]
+and the mixed products vanish because their scalar factor is
+$s/d+e=0$. In $\rho_R(T_a')\rho_R(T_b)$ the $T_kT_k'$ coefficient
+is $\chi(a-b,k)(1/d+K_R(b-a)/N)=\delta_{a,b}$; the
+$S_hS_h'$ coefficient is $\delta_{a,b}$ by orthogonality. Thus this
+product too is $\delta_{a,b}$.
+
+There is a coefficient error in the displayed completeness calculation in
+\cite[Appendix~B.1, printed p.~30]{RW2}: the coefficient of $P_S$ is
+printed as $N(N+d)/d^2$. Direct multiplication of
+\eqref{eq:ring-rho-S}--\eqref{eq:ring-rho-Tprime} instead gives
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:correct-completeness-coefficient}
+ \frac N{d^2}+\frac Nd=\frac{N(1+d)}{d^2}=1.
+\end{equation}
+The two contributions are respectively from the $S$-sum and the $T$-sum.
+After the mixed monomials cancel, the full expression is
+\begin{align*}
+ &\sum_g\rho_R(S_g)\rho_R(S_g')+\sum_g\rho_R(T_g)\rho_R(T_g')\\
+ &\quad=\left(\frac N{d^2}+\frac Nd\right)P_S
+      +\sum_kT_kP_ST_k'\\
+ &\qquad+\sum_{h,k,r}\frac{q(h)}{q(r)}
+       \left(\frac1d+\frac1NK_R(h-r)\right)
+       T_{h+k}T_{-h}T_{-r}'T_{r+k}'\\
+ &\quad=P_S+\sum_kT_kP_ST_k'+\sum_kT_kP_TT_k'=1.
+\end{align*}
+Consequently $\rho_R$ is a well-defined unital endomorphism over $R$.
+The corrected calculation proves the required identity; it does not change
+the generator formulas or the construction in \cite{RW2}.
+
+\paragraph{Covariance and the fusion identity.}
+The $\alpha$ and $U$ identities and \eqref{eq:ring-covariance} follow by
+multiplying the displayed formulas and reindexing finite sums. To make
+explicit why the longer fusion verification also applies over $R$, we
+record its reductions from \cite[Appendix~B.2]{RW2}. First they give
+\[
+ \rho_R(U_g)=\sum_hS_{h-g}S_h'
+        +\sum_h\frac{q(h+g)}{q(h)}T_{h+g}U_gT_h'.
+\]
+Its mixed matrix coefficients vanish by the Fourier relation; its
+$S$-coefficients use \eqref{eq:correct-completeness-coefficient}, and its
+$T$-coefficients use \eqref{eq:ring-correlation}. The subsequent
+calculations for $\rho_R^2(S_g)$, $\rho_R^2(U_g)$, and
+$\rho_R^2(x)S_g=S_g\alpha_g(x)$ use these identities, covariance, and
+the same Fourier and correlation relations.
+
+For the remaining $T$-calculation put
+$\widehat T_g=s^{-1}\sum_h\chi(g,h)T_h$. In the key identity
+\[
+ \rho_R(T_0'\rho_R(T_0))\widehat T_0
+       =\rho_R(T_0')\widehat T_0\rho_R(T_0),
+\]
+the three coefficient comparisons are the following:
+\begin{align*}
+ \lambda q(h)E(h)&=s^{-1}\sum_kE(k)\chi(h,-k),\\
+ \frac\lambda N\sum_hq(h)^2\chi(h,-k)E(-h)E(k-h)
+     &=\frac{\lambda^2}s-\frac{\lambda^{-1}}{dq(k)},\\
+ q(x-y)E(x)E(y)&=-\lambda^{-1}e+
+     s^{-1}\sum_hq(y-h)E(y-h)E(h)q(x-h)E(x-h).
+\end{align*}
+The first is Fourier, and the third is \eqref{eq:ring-dual-pentagon}.
+For the second, Fourier inversion reduces its left side to
+\[
+ \frac{\lambda^{-1}}{Nq(k)}\sum_rE(r)E(-r)\chi(r,k).
+\]
+Now use reflection,
+$s^{-1}\sum_rq(r)^{-1}\chi(r,k)=\Gamma q(k)$,
+$\Gamma=\lambda^3$, and $N/d^2-1=-N/d$.
+
+All steps in these reductions are identities of finite sums in $R$.
+For completeness, the cancellation at the end of the argument is also
+valid over a ring with zero divisors: if
+$\rho_R(S_g')v=\rho_R(T_g')v=0$ for every $g$, then
+\[
+ v=\sum_g\rho_R(S_g)\rho_R(S_g')v+
+          \sum_g\rho_R(T_g)\rho_R(T_g')v=0.
+\]
+Applied to the remaining difference, the preceding identities and
+covariance give
+$\rho_R^2(T_0)\widehat T_g=\widehat T_g\rho_R(T_0)$ for every $g$.
+The finite Fourier matrix is invertible over $R$, so this gives the same
+identity with $T_g$ instead of $\widehat T_g$. Together with
+$T_h=U_{-h}T_0$ and the already verified $\rho_R^2(U_{-h})$ identity,
+it gives
+$\rho_R^2(T_h)T_g=T_g\rho_R(T_h)$. These are precisely the remaining
+split fusion equations. Completeness proves \eqref{eq:ring-fusion} on
+unprimed generators. Equality on the primed generators follows from
+uniqueness of their left inverses subject to completeness, again without
+cancelling a nonunit.
+
+Finally \eqref{eq:ring-snake} follows at once from
+\eqref{eq:ring-rho-S} and the Leavitt relations. Thus the fusion and
